## 12. Delivery plan

### Phase 0 — Reproduce and instrument (1–2 days)

- Reproduce the Inbox route failure from a real widget.
- Record incoming native payload/URI in debug logs.
- Document current Flutter route definitions and initialization sequence.
- Add a test that fails on the current `orbit://inbox/` behavior.

### Phase 1 — Navigation fix and safe fallback (2–3 days)

- Add centralized widget action resolver.
- Normalize/validate canonical URIs or move widgets to command payloads.
- Map actions to approved internal routes.
- Add top-level safe unknown-route behavior for widget origin.
- Verify cold/warm/foreground navigation.

### Phase 2 — Data/action hardening (2–4 days)

- Add idempotent completion processing.
- Implement versioned atomic widget snapshots and last-good fallback.
- Refresh all affected widget instances after task mutations.
- Add stale/deleted-task handling.

### Phase 3 — Diagnostics and full QA (2–4 days)

- Add required structured logs and debug diagnostics panel.
- Complete unit, integration, and manual matrix testing.
- Run full existing-app regression tests.
- Release to internal testers before production rollout.

---

## 13. Definition of done

This remediation is complete only when the Inbox widget shown in the supplied evidence opens the real Inbox screen reliably, every other widget tap follows the same safe contract, and no widget interaction can expose application internals to a user.

The finished experience should feel delightfully boring in the best possible way: tap a widget, get exactly where you expected, and carry on with your day.
